**A build consumes time and workspace capacity.** Long or duplicate builds can delay other teams and increase usage. Inspect the actual plan and workflow settings before estimating capacity or changing limits.

**Work location — authorized Bitrise workspace settings, build results, and usage/billing records.** Use [Operations Overview](./index.mdx) to find the other maintenance guides.

## 1. Confirm the actual limits

The latest supplied reference records the following values. Its timeout and retention statements require confirmation against the active enterprise plan and configuration.

| Area | Value described in the supplied reference | What the owner must confirm |
|---|---|---|
| Bitrise build timeout | 90 minutes. | Actual timeout for the selected workspace, runner, and workflow. |
| Configuration-repository timeout | A separate recorded run uses 55 minutes. | The limit enforced by its Vela/configuration execution environment. |
| Concurrency | Maximum simultaneous VMs on the referenced organization tier. | Current runner capacity and queue behavior. |
| Artifact retention | 30 days. | Artifact-type retention and any enterprise custom policy. |
| macOS initialization | 1–3 minutes. | Observed startup delay for the actual selected stack and run. |

Current public Bitrise limits depend on the plan. Enterprise configuration is custom. Use the actual enterprise agreement/settings to resolve the recorded timeout and capacity values. Public documentation was checked on **8 November 2026**. [Bitrise plans](https://bitrise.io/pricing)

### Retention requires explicit confirmation

The public artifact policy checked on that date lists these periods for its current policy.

| Artifact type | Public retention period |
|---|---|
| Test results and non-installable artifacts | 90 days. |
| Installable APK/AAB/IPA artifacts | 150 days. |
| HTML reports | 7 days. |
| Pipeline intermediate files | 7 days. |

Enterprise customers can have custom retention for eligible artifact types. Confirm whether the reference's 30-day period describes your own policy. Preserve required release evidence in approved storage before its applicable retention expires. [Artifact retention policy](https://docs.bitrise.io/en/bitrise-ci/run-and-analyze-builds/managing-build-files/artifact-retention-policy)

## 2. Find why a build is slow

1. Open the exact build and record its workflow, source commit, runner/stack, and duration.
2. Distinguish queue/startup delay from commands already executing.
3. Inspect step timings for checkout, dependencies, tests, build/archive, cloud assessment, and delivery.
4. Identify the longest relevant phase and its recorded failure or wait state.
5. Agree the configuration change with the owner and compare the affected phase after the reviewed change.

Use the timeout of the platform involved in the failure. A Vela apply timeout and a Bitrise app-build timeout refer to separate executions.

**Attach screenshot — step timings, selected runner, and relevant configured limit with sensitive details hidden.**

## 3. Understand the earlier credit example

The earlier supplied reference describes **30,000 credits per month**, an assumed **30 credits per build**, and **USD 14,500 per year**. Its effective contract date and the active enterprise billing model are unspecified. Use these as recorded calculation inputs and confirm current billing with the workspace owner.

| Calculation | Result using those assumptions |
|---|---|
| Monthly build capacity | `30,000 / 30 = 1,000` builds. |
| Monthly equivalent of the annual cost | `14,500 / 12 = USD 1,208.33` approximately. |
| Allocated cost per build at full assumed capacity | `14,500 / (12 × 1,000) = USD 1.21` approximately. |

The earlier figures of USD 1,215 per month, USD 14.40 per build, and USD 0.48 per build per day do not follow from those inputs. The table provides the corrected arithmetic.

The allocated USD 1.21 assumes all 1,000 monthly builds are used and the annual cost covers the modeled service. It leaves actual extra fees and changing usage to the current agreement. Obtain the real billing model before applying this estimate to a budget.

## 4. Count workflow runs and routes correctly

One source change can start several workflows or routes. Confirm how the active contract measures those executions and charges their usage.

The four governed routes are `qa_android`, `qa_ios`, `uat_android`, and `uat_ios`. Their duration and consumption can differ. An average for one build cannot establish the cost of the whole release until every applicable run is accounted for.

For a release estimate, collect these actual measurements.

| Measurement | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Runs started by the change | Shows the full fan-out from PR, push, tag, schedule, and route triggers. |
| Runner/stack for each run | Identifies the execution resource used. |
| Duration and reported usage | Provides the measured basis for capacity/cost analysis. |
| Retries and duplicate triggers | Identifies extra executions associated with the change. |
| Device/security service usage | Keeps separate service consumption visible when estimating total release cost. |

**Expected result —** the owner can connect an estimate to measured runs and the confirmed billing contract.

## 5. Reduce avoidable build usage

The supplied governance guidance calls for local checks, elimination of redundant builds, and failure investigation before reruns.

| Situation | Action |
|---|---|
| Application already fails locally | Fix the affected local check before using another cloud run. |
| PR and push events launch duplicate work | Have the owner review the actual trigger mapping and required status coverage. |
| Module tests can execute independently | Use the reviewed detection and output handoff in [Parallel Testing](../parallel-testing.mdx). |
| Dependency preparation is repeatedly expensive | Inspect the approved cache strategy, keys, restore/save behavior, and measured result. |
| Long device tests occupy build capacity | Use the approved suite split or Sauce Labs integration while preserving required result collection. |
| A run fails repeatedly | Inspect and correct its first relevant failure before another execution. |

The supplied material gives no complete cache configuration or guaranteed percentage saving. Use the selected template's actual cache contract and compare observed duration and consumption.

## 6. Preserve evidence and handle operational failures

Have the platform owner confirm the artifact/evidence retention requirements, archive destination, retrieval procedure, and access policy. Verify required artifacts are accessible through the approved mechanism before depending on them for later recovery.

| Problem | Next action |
|---|---|
| Build is queued | Inspect current runner capacity, running jobs, and actual plan limits. |
| Build timed out | Identify the execution platform, its effective limit, and last completed operation. |
| Usage exceeds the estimate | Compare the measured run count, duration/resource selection, retries, and billing period. |
| Artifact expired or retrieval failed | Inspect approved retention/storage records and follow reviewed recovery using existing identities. |
| Faster configuration skips a required result | Restore the required coverage and have the owner review the optimization. |

For partial or unknown external outcomes, use [Troubleshooting & Recovery](../troubleshooting-and-recovery.mdx).

